<!--
RULE.md template
================

A rule is a constraint the agent must respect, together with the
reason it exists. If what you are writing has steps, it is a skill,
not a rule; use SKILL.md.template instead.

Keep rules single-purpose. One constraint per file. A rule whose
title uses "and" is usually two rules bundled together and should
be split.

Keep the body under 100 lines. If it is longer, you probably have
more than one rule and should split before doing anything else.

The three body sections below are all mandatory. Every one of them
carries specific weight; skipping any weakens the rule to the point
where it stops working.
-->

# {Short imperative restating the rule}

<!--
Mandatory. The first paragraph must be a single sentence in EARS
form: "When X, the system shall Y, because Z."

- "When X" is the trigger. Without it, the agent cannot tell which
  situations the rule applies to
- "The system shall Y" is the constraint. "Shall" is deliberately
  archaic; it distinguishes a normative statement from "may",
  "should", or "will"
- "Because Z" is the reason. Without it, the agent must obey blindly
  or violate blindly when it hits a case you did not foresee

Do not omit any of the three parts.
-->

When {trigger condition}, the system shall {constraint}, because
{reason the constraint matters}.

## Signals of violation
<!--
Mandatory. Concrete things a reviewer looking at a diff would see.
Each bullet describes what the reviewer would spot in the code.
Vague signals like "code that looks wrong" defeat the purpose;
the goal is a list an automated reviewer or a junior contributor
could apply mechanically.

At least two bullets. A single signal is usually a symptom of a
broader pattern that deserves naming as its own bullet.
-->

- {Concrete observable signal in the code}
- {Concrete observable signal in the code}

## How to satisfy it
<!--
Mandatory. The pattern that makes the code conform to the rule. If
a helper, wrapper, or utility exists that satisfies the rule, name
it and say where it lives. If the pattern is inline, show a two
or three line snippet.

"Just don't do the wrong thing" is not actionable and does not
count. The section must tell the agent what to do instead.
-->

{Description of the pattern that satisfies the rule, or the name
and location of the helper that does.}
